model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "The disabled runtime-slot controller owns active selection in runtime-slots-v1 storage beside existing ownership and membership records. The live stack evaluates no extra resource, exports no runtime class, and adds no binding, migration or release declaration. Promotion also binds the current controller release and source input independently of stable controller and runtime identity. Exact execution identity scopes stream accounting and health watchers across repeated use of the same logical slot. A future private slot driver must bind provider application, version, logical instance, immutable image and process boot separately from the stable controller identity. Candidate startup never calls the active runtime lifecycle. Promotion collects fresh health and every current native-policy acknowledgement, dirty or clean, outside storage transactions, then compares complete membership and selection in one transaction and commits selection with its receipt. Expired candidate acknowledgements cannot borrow a renewed desired deadline. Retired membership denials stay in the policy digest and promotion revision receipt, and every candidate must acknowledge their fresh dirty empty projections. Clean live policies must be unexpired. A dirty live policy, such as a pool that is not ready, vetoes only that pool in the runtime scheduler and slot router; runtime-wide proofs and recovery install it with a fresh lease, and its revision stays in the digest compared at commit. An uncertain started promotion permits inspection only. Admission leases register before authority or signing and identify their selected slot before dispatch. Each runtime's readiness may report its measured distance to the database, location and measurement state; the health read carries them as closed values and an image without them is unknown. Model routing prefers runtimes within twenty milliseconds of the database while they are under the growth target, then far and unknown runtimes under it, then the rest; without distances the order is unchanged and no ready runtime becomes unroutable. Ready cells take every model call and the root serves one only when no ready cell can: the root's image is pinned and a Home fences a peer node whose liveness is three seconds old, so a burst on the root must never refuse a call a cell could serve. When no cell is ready, the root takes new model calls at a capped rate, a token bucket of 1.2 a second with a burst of 10, and refuses the rest with the retryable answer, sending nothing and still growing the fleet. The refusal is marked unsent, so the entry sends a held call again each second inside its replay window. That keeps its own Home's heartbeat and master duties alive. Catalog reads, which the fleet already retries, take no token, a call that fails before its send gives its token back, and no more than a burst of calls holding a token may wait for a root that is still starting. The figures come from the root's old Home (heartbeat gap 8.9 s at 1.5 a second, 11.9 s at 2.5, broken at about 3; bursts of 18 and 30 absorbed) and are re-derived when the root gets a new image. The root's calls stay counted reservations either way, so growth still reads them after the controller restarts. Fleet rows record the reported distance for the fleet read, active-health carries the root's, and each cell admission logs one closed placement decision. A started cell more than twenty milliseconds away is redrawn only while another runtime serves, fewer than four draws were made and the fleet doesn't need it now: it moves from warming straight to draining, so no request is routed to it, and the ordinary zero-activity drain and retirement stop it; it is never hard-killed. The root counts as serving only on this controller's own latest successful health read of it. Before retirement the redraw is re-checked, and a redrawn cell whose justifying runtime stopped serving returns to ready. Replacements, planned or for quarantine, are never redrawn. A lone far cell, and the fourth far draw, serve. A far cell measured within fifty milliseconds is needed now, and serves at once, when another cell already serves and fewer cells are ready than the demand of that moment wants, or, with only the root serving, when the root's fallback cap turned a call away within the last five seconds; it leaves by the ordinary idle-out. Admission is idempotent per boot. The draw count is kept in the fleet's durable storage, raised by each redraw and cleared by each admission, so a controller reset can't restart it; a count whose last redraw is older than two start bounds starts over, and an unreadable one starts over too. Revocation persists dirty state and aborts pool streams before all active, candidate and draining slots acknowledge, or an explicit durable controller fence denies routing and closes that slot's streams. A fence records when it was set. Unfence proves the slot's health and current policies first; an active slot that can't prove them goes back to recovery instead, unproven. Its own refusal is told from the controller's by the endpoint's closed reason; a controller or endpoint refusal, or a defect, keeps the fence. A fence stops membership renewing, so once its clean leases ran out the slot goes back to recovery without a proof. A fence stops the fleet alarm that runs recovery, so a successful unfence arms it again. An unproven slot takes Home and management calls, the way current policies reach it, but no model call, and nothing reports it serving until current policies reach its boot, by its proof or recovery's restart. Its health read still pins the attested boot on every call. A fenced boot can still hold clean grants from before its fence for the native plugin's 24-hour grace, so that gate, not the fence's age, is what keeps them unused. The unfence reply names the path and carries the recovery state; the operator inspect shows the path and whether the slot is proven. An unknown or unfenced non-acknowledging slot prevents writer acknowledgement. Fleet cells settle a membership install one by one: a retiring or quarantined cell gets none, a draining cell that can't return to ready settles its refusal, and a ready or redrawn draining cell whose runtime fails the install is quarantined so it takes no call under its old policy. A starting cell's refusal still holds acknowledgement, because it could be admitted under the old policy, and so does a refusal from a cell's policy layer, which the root gives too. Every fleet row records when it entered its phase, and each phase has a bound: a start 240 s, the startup allowance plus activation; a drain whose count stays unknown or unreadable 300 s; a retirement whose provider absence isn't proven 60 s. Past its bound a row becomes quarantined with a closed reason. A start its endpoint refused before the durable first-start intent was written reached no container, and that object would refuse a retry, so its row is quarantined at once as an unproven start, with the refusal's step, reason and cause, rather than holding growth for the whole bound; a refusal for identity or for the controller's code is the exception, because for a fresh slot it is about the endpoint's own deployment or the signed start itself (its application binding, controller code, runtime configuration, signing key or the start's freshness), never the slot, as while the controller still runs an upload a rollback re-pointed away from, so another fresh slot fares no better: that row leaves as never started once its cell's abandon proves nothing ran, with no quarantine, its recent end carrying the refusal's read under a deployment rule that never counts against a runtime, logs one bounded deferred-start line, frees its place for growth, and growth waits at least until the next upkeep pass before drawing again, while a cell that can't prove it keeps the quarantine; a start that may have been sent, whose intent can't be read, or that failed locally before any send stays uncertain as before and retries the same slot. At its start bound, one its cell proves was never sent (no start under way, no first-start intent, its endpoint fenced) leaves as never started, with no quarantine, its recent end under an unsent rule that never counts against a runtime, and logs one bounded unsent-start line; any other stays quarantined as an unproven start. A quarantined row takes no call and holds its physical place until its cell proves the instance cannot run, because its endpoint was first fenced durably, refusing every later admission and start for the slot behind any start already running, and then either no durable first-start intent was ever written or the provider reported it absent on every read for a whole start bound, since a listing can lag a start just sent; a destroyed instance listed as inactive counts as absent only while the fenced endpoint reports its container stopped. A quarantined start that never activated has its fenced container stopped once, on a later pass: SIGTERM, then its own exit or the forced-stop grace and destroy, recorded before the signal so no repeated pass, fresh controller or replaced object signals again. Only a fleet cell's own endpoint stops it, behind the fence and in its serialized section, and only for an initial start; never the root, a start with an activated first start or a selection, or a row with an identity, and never while the cell's own start or activation is still returning. An attestation the endpoint recorded before the fence doesn't spare it: behind the fence every attestation, read and call for the slot refuses, so a selection that commits after the cell's read serves nothing, and its container is stopped like any other. Its place is then free within the start bound, the stop grace and the absence bound. A start its bound quarantined is never admitted, however late it activates, so it takes no call whether or not its slot was fenced. Once it activated, it is fenced but never stopped here: the fence ends its endpoint's renewals, so its container stops at its inactivity timeout, and it frees its place once its endpoint reports that container stopped and the provider agrees for the absence bound. An endpoint on older code fences without stopping, and the next pass asks again. An abandon and a start never overlap on one cell. No bound releases a place on time alone, and an endpoint never admits a slot whose record it retired, so a retired slot never starts again. Quarantine logs one line on entry and one each hour it stays, and the fleet inspect shows each row's reason and age, and for a start held without an identity how far its first start got: activated (its selection committed), attested (its endpoint recorded the attestation and nothing was selected) or neither. That is a read of the cell's first-start intent, which the selection's own commit marks, and of its endpoint's record, which a fenced slot still answers, and it never fences, stops, starts or renews anything; a cell that can't say, as an endpoint on older code, reads null, and the fleet lane prints null for any other value, any other row and a controller on older code, which sends none. Every row that leaves keeps a recent end saying how it left and, for a quarantine, why, including a cancelled reservation and a start a new generation retires; the fleet lane prints each row's slot key, the one its recent end carries, and its ordinal. A refused growth logs the runtime's step, reason and cause and the error's class, never its text, once until any of them changes. A bound adds no replacement demand; growth instead counts no quarantined row toward the cells it wants. A ledger count is lowered to the cell's own stream count, never below it, only once both have stood still 300 s, the cell's measured where it is kept, and that count is read without draining the cell, so a live stream of any length stays counted and a release still on its way is never undercut. Fleet upkeep settles each row on its own and always runs growth, and the idle drain keeps the last runtime that can take a call while the root can't. Private runtime request boundaries decode invalid input and identity into typed refusals with closed reasons. A caller running other controller code than the endpoint or the handoff bridge is refused as controller_code, apart from identity, since every release changes that code; a caller on older code reads that unknown reason as no diagnostic and an uncertain refusal, never a defect. A fleet first start whose endpoint runs other controller code than its controller adopts the controller's: the controller names its own code on the witness read, and the endpoint answers adopting with its own code and the first adoption's time, then resets its object once that answer has left, as the controller's code handover resets the controller, so the next pass meets the deployed code. An attestation signed by other code is refused as controller_code and adopts the same way. It never adopts a start with an attestation, one with admitted streams, or the root's, and resets at most once each 30 s, recorded in its own storage with the code it left and the first adoption's time, so an instance the platform still serves the old code waits that window out instead of resetting in a loop. An endpoint on older code ignores the field and never adopts, so its start still waits for its bound. A first start decides after one observation otherwise: one whose witness differs from what its controller signs for in anything but the code (the operation, manifest, configuration fingerprint, target or a missing boot), one whose endpoint refuses to adopt, and one still answering adopting a window after its first adoption, are quarantined at once as unproven starts with that read, and the stop of never-activated starts then stops them; an attestation refused for its code only fails that pass, and the next witness decides. Unexpected defects return a sanitized correlated 500, retain the cause class only in logs, and cross the private binding without becoming refusals or repeating mutations. Signed admission does not require a forwarding identity header. Drivers have no public ingress. Per-slot health leases protect draining streams independently. Retirement waits for drain, targets the exact old boot and retains uncertainty without repeating destruction. Pre-adoption legacy request counts belong to the isolate that holds them, so a count left by a reset or redeployed isolate never holds a drain open; established legacy connections stay the drain proof. At most one inactive candidate or draining slot coexists with active serving. Switching back requires the same fresh policy and identity proof. Existing lifecycle code ignores the additive namespace. Provisioning, private runtime adapters, release admission and live reliance require their separate approved phase."
    }
  }
}
